  render unstyled.

  This looks bounded. It adds filters to a page that already exists, so I'll present a short design here in chat rather
  than write a spec. Say so if you'd rather I take the heavier path.

  [1mFirst question: which ways of narrowing the table should we build?[0m This decides which controls go on the page and how
  big the diff gets.

  - [1mEvent type plus text search (my recommendation).[0m The type filter is a checkbox list of the nine types, and the
    search box matches device and location. Failed sign-ins are one checkbox away. This covers the support tickets we've
    had. It's about two controls' worth of diff, and a date range can be added later without redoing anything.
  - [1mEvent type only.[0m This is the smallest diff and still makes failed sign-ins easy to find. You couldn't ask "anything
    from Frankfurt?" or "anything from the API client?", though search can be added later at low cost.
  - [1mEvent type, search and a date range.[0m This is the most complete option. The date range brings two date pickers,
    presets, the [38;5;153mdate_order[39m validation and time zone edge cases at the day boundary, so it's roughly double the diff and
    may push toward the 300-line split. For only 90 days of data, it adds little over the newest-first ordering.
  - [1mA "Failed sign-ins only" shortcut.[0m You can add this to any option above. It's a one-click toggle on top of the type
    filter. It's cheap, but it's a second way to do the same thing, and stronger failed-sign-in treatment is on the
    follow-up list pending Security's input.

  I recommend event type plus search. It answers the questions people are actually asking and keeps the diff small. Of
  everything above, only the date range carries real cost if you add it later.
